#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097751} :activity/id #uuid "f055ed71-ae5b-569a-8cbe-acd4850a5cce" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049507 :topic/id #uuid "fa777873-dae5-503a-8b8d-d333b158b938" :topic/math-academy-id 1080} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097751 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097803 :tutorial/content "Suppose that we want to *approximate*\n\n$$\nf(x) = e^x\n$$\n\nfor some value of $x$. We can do this using the second-degree Taylor polynomial about a point $x=a$. For example, the second-degree Taylor polynomial for $e^x$ about the point $x=1$ is shown below:\n\n$$\ne^x \\approx P_2(x) = e + e(x-1) + \\dfrac 1 2 e(x-1)^2\n$$\n\nIn general, the polynomial $P_2(x)$ is only an approximation to the function $f(x)$, which means that there will usually be some error whenever we use $P_2$ to approximate $f$. We want to know how bad this error can get. Can we find an upper bound for the error?\n\nLet $R_2(x)$ be the **error** (or **remainder**) that we get when we use $P_2$ to approximate\n\n$$\nf(x)=e^x\n$$\n\nso\n\n$$\nR_2(x) = e^x - P_2(x)\n$$\n\nThe **Lagrange error bound** states that an upper bound for $R_2$ is given by,\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}R_2 & \\leq \\dfrac{M}{3!}\\end{vmatrix}x-\\left.a\\right|^{3}\n$$\n\nwhere $M$ the maximum value of $\\mid f^{(3)}(x) \\mid$ on the interval between $a$ and $x$.\n\nIn our case, since our Taylor polynomial is taken about the point $a=1$, the Lagrange error bound becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}R_2 & \\leq \\dfrac{M}{3!}\\end{vmatrix}x-\\left.1\\right|^{3}\n$$\n\nNotice that the Lagrange error bound looks a lot like the last term of the *next* Taylor polynomial, $P_3(x)$.\n\nThe tricky part is finding the value of $M$. We'll learn how to do that in the following example." :tutorial/id #uuid "fcb4053a-c61a-538b-8b44-5a92f73f053f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1292 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "c93a74b3-e513-5fb9-9595-6bf177ec940a" :step/math-academy-id 4844 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097752}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097752 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096820 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096562 :question/id #uuid "c946c1c9-f3d2-5d9d-9390-8ee542ca15a5" :question/math-academy-id "e-745"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "360b8076-1b27-5630-a07e-221a0e53011d" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048515} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050718} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050866} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050955}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096971} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097317}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Upper Bound for the Error Using the Second-Degree Taylor Polynomial"} :step/id #uuid "82542d7a-3ec1-5979-8dd2-13a732ad5eb1" :step/math-academy-id 4845 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097753}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097753 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097804 :tutorial/content "The Lagrange error bound that we've discussed so far is valid only for the error associated with a second-degree Taylor polynomial. But we can generalize the result to higher-order Taylor polynomials.\n\nLet $R_n(x)$ be the error involved in approximating a function $f(x)$ by its $n$th-degree Taylor polynomial $P_n(x)$ about $x=a$. So, we have\n\n$$\nR_n(x) = f(x) - P_n(x)\n$$\n\nThen the Lagrange error bound for $R_n$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}R_n(x) & \\leq \\dfrac{M}{(n+1)!}\\end{vmatrix}x-\\left.a\\right|^{n+1}\n$$\n\nwhere $M$ is the maximum value of $\\mid f^{(n+1)} \\mid$ on the interval between $a$ and $x$." :tutorial/id #uuid "07b10e6a-1673-5d6e-8572-8b4505579f44" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1294 :tutorial/title "The General Result of the Lagrange Error Bound"} :step/id #uuid "13415159-b315-5389-bc81-61ef044820e2" :step/math-academy-id 4849 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097754}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097754 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096821 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096563 :question/id #uuid "3f5e5945-cdaa-55b9-b360-9e68249480b7" :question/math-academy-id "e-746"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "79a10073-8798-597e-821c-691b0dad5ae7" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096961} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096980} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527920}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Upper Bound for the Error Using the Third-Degree Taylor Polynomial"} :step/id #uuid "b70fcb39-7efc-50ad-a28c-73509579c61d" :step/math-academy-id 4850}] :activity/title "The Lagrange Error Bound" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186096869}]}
